[Insight Discovery]: The substitution \(x=dX,\ y=dY\) isolates the common scale \(d=\gcd(x,y)\). This matters because the original fraction’s dependence on \(d\) becomes explicit.

[Operation Formulation]: Substitute \(x=dX\) and \(y=dY\) into the given equation, then simplify the common factors of \(d\).

[Operation Execution]:
\[
\frac{(dX)(dY)^3}{dX+dY}
=\frac{d^4XY^3}{d(X+Y)}
=\frac{d^3XY^3}{X+Y}
=p.
\]

[Insight Discovery]: The bound \(2\le n\le 9\) is only a necessary range, not the solution set. Since it contains finitely many integers, it can now be checked directly using \(\dfrac{n^3-n}{2^n+n}\).

[Operation Formulation]: Substitute each \(n=2,3,\ldots,9\) into \(\dfrac{n^3-n}{2^n+n}\) and keep only the values that are integers.

[Operation Execution]:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
n&2&3&4&5&6&7&8&9\\ \hline
\dfrac{n^3-n}{2^n+n}
&1&\frac{24}{11}&3&\frac{120}{37}&3&
\frac{112}{45}&\frac{21}{11}&\frac{720}{521}
\end{array}
\]
Thus, within this range, the integer-valued cases are \(\boxed{n=2,4,6}\).

[Insight Discovery]: All three numbers share a factor of \(10\), so their cubes share the factor \(10^3\). Factoring this out makes the large numbers manageable. Since the terms are added, do not split the radical over the addition signs.

[Operation Formulation]: Factor \(10^3\) from all three cubic terms.

[Operation Execution]:  
\[
\sqrt{30^3+40^3+50^3}
=\sqrt{10^3(3^3+4^3+5^3)}
=\sqrt{1000(27+64+125)}
=\sqrt{216000}.
\]
